      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "To avoid sudden collapses, it's essential to install deck components properly and maintain the structure regularly. Signs that a deck may be failing include obvious cracking or leaning of joists, sagging between supports, loose or brittle deck boards, or tripping on hidden voids. Periodically inspect the deck, checking for nails or screws that have come loose, and ensure that your decking support system (joists and beams) has not deteriorated. Pay attention to the surrounding soil conditions underneath the deck, as excessive moisture can weaken the support. If you notice the aforementioned signs, consult a structural engineer immediately to address any potentially critical issues. Keeping a record of all inspections and repairs can also help you track the deck's condition over time and preemptively identify any emerging problems before they lead to a failure. For peace of mind, consistently follow the local building codes and use high-quality, durable materials that exceed the minimum requirements for your location. Implementing a regular maintenance schedule is key to preserving the longevity and stability of your deck. Additionally, sometimes preemptive deck reinforcement or retrofitting can be considered in areas with high wind or seismic activity to add an extra layer of safety. Engaging a professional with expertise in deck construction can offer valuable insights into",
